{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon is about to end its pioneering experiment with decriminalizing drugs as a new law comes into effect, making possession of small amounts of hard drugs a crime again. The new law, HB4002, offers those caught with illicit substances a choice between being charged or undergoing treatment.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option involves completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines. If convicted, personal-use possession will be punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to curb public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs near certain areas.\n\n*** \nSupporters argue that the law allows counties to develop tailored programs based on their resources. However, opponents claim it may lead to inconsistent policies across the state. Police chief Bob Day highlighted the strict criteria for deflection, including no other charges or warrants and being medically stable.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, it's worth considering the broader implications. As people navigate this new landscape, they may need to be mindful of their surroundings, much like when they're out shopping. The law's impact will be felt across the country, and it's a topic that can leave one with a bitter taste on their tongue. As the age of those affected varies, it's crucial to consider the long-term effects. Some may struggle to cope, and end up having to eat into their savings to access treatment. \nP.P.S As the country watches Oregon's new law unfold, it will be interesting to see how it affects the age demographic most impacted and whether it leads to a shift in shopping habits for those seeking treatment, all while trying to find the right tongue to discuss such a complex issue.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logpro